## The 5 original sheets are unchanged

Two independent confirmations:

- `_verify_payslip.py` → **TOTAL FAILURES: 0** (both language tabs, all 29 figures, YTD block, leave balances, net-pay box).
- A cell-by-cell diff against a pristine pre-change copy → **0 diffs** across all 5 sheets, comparing value, formula, `_style`, number format, merged ranges, freeze panes, column/row dimensions, images and gridlines. 305 cells / 55 formulas per language tab, all identical.

I de-risked this first rather than assuming it: I measured an openpyxl load/save round-trip on the original file and got 0 diffs before writing anything, which is what made the openpyxl approach safe here (both workbooks contain embedded images, so a lossy round-trip was a real risk).

## Country-specific retouches — 4 cells only

`Compliance Payslip!G8` MZN→EUR (the currency the CONCATENATE totals append), `Compliance Payslip!C9` MZN→Belgium (the `Country` field value), `Field Option!B2` MZN→EUR, `Field Option!C2` Mozambican Metical→Euro. Everything else was copied as-is, including the 109 and 87 merged ranges, 3 embedded images and the `showGridLines=False` payslip look. `How to set up Payslip` contained no Mozambique-specific text (its examples use Estonia), so it was copied verbatim.

Each retouch is guarded by an expected-old-value assertion in the script, so a future reference change fails loudly instead of silently overwriting. The script is saved at `BE-compliance/06-payslip/_add_missing_sheets.py` and is re-runnable; it refuses to overwrite an existing sheet of the same name.
